.TH SESSIONTEST 1
.SH NAME
sessiontest \- check that a recording decodes into the right actions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sessiontest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Sessiontest
checks
.IR session (2)'s
decoding: typing with the releases dropped and a repeated letter kept, a named
key ending a run of text, a pause becoming an explicit wait, a press and
release becoming a click, two of those becoming a double click or not
depending on the 500ms between them, movement with a button held becoming a
drag, and a resize.
.PP
It
.I builds
recordings rather than making them.
That is the point rather than a shortcut: the half of the decoder that matters
most cannot be exercised any other way here.
Clicks, double clicks and drags are what the recorded-time design exists for,
and synthetic clicks from the host do not arrive on this platform \(em see
.IR inputtest (1).
Injecting them through
.B /dev/pointerin
does not help either, because that path deliberately does not record, for the
same reason writing to
.B /dev/keyboard
does not.
A recording cannot contain events the machine put there itself.
.PP
What is left is the decoder's real input, which is a file in a documented
format.
Building one with known events tests exactly what was otherwise going untested.
.SH "WHAT IT FOUND"
Three things, none of which a real recording made obvious.
.PP
That a long pause in the middle of typing was swallowed: "a", two seconds, "b"
decoded as typing
.BR ab ,
losing the pause \(em which is the whole reason times are recorded.
A script that types the right characters looks right.
.PP
That a double click decoded as a click
.I and
a double click, because the first of the pair had already been reported before
the second made it a double.
Harmless to read and not harmless to compile: the script grew by one action
every time it went round.
.PP
That a pointer movement which is the first pointer event of a recording was
swallowed entirely, because the position it would be compared against is seeded
from that very event, so nothing looks like movement.
A recording made by hand has hundreds of movements and a later one always
triggers; a compiled one can have exactly the one.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/sessiontest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR session (2),
.IR sh-session (1),
.IR inputtest (1)
.PP
.B /doc/session.ms
\(em
.IR "Recording and Replaying an Inferno Session" ,
a tutorial covering all of this and how it was checked.
.SH BUGS
It checks decoding and compiling, not performing.
That a recording becomes the right script is tested here; that the script then
delivers the same keystrokes was checked by hand, by running one against a
fresh session and comparing what arrived.
.PP
Nothing here covers a recording made by the real hardware, only recordings in
the format the real hardware produces.
